# DTF tier manifest: empty-repo-key-no-body-read  (artifact-keeper#3509 / #3444)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. The defect is in the auth middleware's
# empty-key branch, ahead of every handler and of any storage backend, so the
# storage dimension is irrelevant.
#
# THE BUG
# #3444 gave `repo_visibility_middleware`'s empty-repository-key branch an early
# return that inserted an anonymous `Option<AuthExtension>` and then ran the
# handler. That early return skips the REST of the middleware — including the
# #508 anonymous-write gate 129 lines further down, which would have refused the
# request. Before #3444 the handler's `Extension<Option<AuthExtension>>`
# extractor rejected FIRST (axum runs `FromRequestParts` before the body's
# `FromRequest`), so the body was never read. Afterwards the handler runs and its
# body extractor (`Bytes`, `Multipart`, ...) buffers the WHOLE request before the
# in-handler `require_auth` can answer 401.
#
# That is an UNAUTHENTICATED MEMORY-EXHAUSTION primitive: no credential, no
# repository, no configuration. Per request the ceiling is `MAX_UPLOAD_SIZE`
# (10 GiB default); concurrently `GLOBAL_MAX_CONCURRENCY` (512 default). When it
# was found, 22 of 36 probed format prefixes buffered a 64 MiB anonymous body and
# a single anonymous 4 GiB PUT took the backend from 35 MiB to 7,045 MiB RSS.
# `POST /conda/t/upload` is a SINGLE-slash path, so a reverse proxy that
# collapses `//` does not filter it, and `/lfs/` is in `BYTE_TRANSFER_PREFIXES`
# and therefore exempt from the global request timeout, so that arm is
# drip-feedable.
#
# THE FIX (#3509): the empty-key branch answers the existence-hiding 404
# directly. An empty key names no repository — keys are non-empty by
# construction — so nothing can be served there, the handler is never invoked,
# and no body is read. #3443's unauthenticated 500 stays closed for the stronger
# reason that the strict extension extractor is never reached.
#
# WHY A REPOSITORY LITERALLY KEYED `t` IS PART OF THE TIER
# `extract_repo_key` skipped the conda token-channel pair `t/<TOKEN>` whenever a
# conda path's second segment was `t` — including two-segment paths like
# `/conda/t/upload` and `/conda/t/channeldata.json`, which are the PLAIN conda
# routes for a repository whose key is literally `t`. Those resolved to an EMPTY
# key, so that repository got no visibility, token-scope or permission check and
# the caller's credential was replaced with the anonymous value: an admin read of
# its own private channel answered 401 for a credential it did send. #3509
# applies the same shape test to both `extract_repo_key` and
# `extract_conda_url_token` — the token channel is a token channel only when a
# segment follows the token. So this tier creates a real repository keyed `t`
# and asserts it is properly authorized, alongside a real token channel that
# must keep working.
#
# THE INSTRUMENT
# `curl -w '%{http_code} %{size_upload}'`. curl sends `Expect: 100-continue` for
# a body this size, so a backend that answers before polling the body leaves
# `size_upload` at exactly 0, and one that polls it uploads all of it. A gate
# that only asserted "0 bytes" would be unfalsifiable, so the tier includes a
# POSITIVE CONTROL on the same run: an AUTHENTICATED upload of the SAME payload
# to a real repository must report the full byte count. If the instrument were
# broken, that control reads 0 and the tier fails there instead of passing
# everything vacuously.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (CONTROL)  the instrument works — an authenticated upload of the same body
#              reports the full `size_upload`. No empty-key path answers an
#              unauthenticated 5xx (the pre-#3444 behaviour #3509 must not
#              reintroduce). A real conda token channel still serves
#              `/conda/t/<TOKEN>/<repo>/...` and a bogus token there is still
#              401. An ordinary conda repository is unaffected.
#   (BOUNDARY) every probed empty-key format prefix answers 401/404 with
#              `size_upload == 0`. The probe set is exactly the prefixes measured
#              to buffer on the vulnerable image, so no gate here is vacuous.
#              RED on `71052767^1`: 401 after uploading the whole 32 MiB.
#   (BOUNDARY) the repository keyed literally `t` is authorized on its own
#              terms — anonymous 401, credentialed 200 — and flipping it
#              public<->private flips the anonymous answer. RED on the baseline
#              for the credentialed read: the empty-key branch discarded the
#              credential, so even an admin got 401 on its own private channel.
#
# Every curl uses --path-as-is. It is mandatory here rather than merely prudent:
# curl normalises `//` and dot segments by default, and the empty segment IS the
# probe. Without it these requests never reach the branch under test.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives an admin login, three repository
# creates, a token mint, a user create + login and ~35 back-to-back probes;
# the base default (ON) would throttle them and turn a boundary 401 into a 429,
# which reads as a pass for the wrong reason. A tier that must disable a shipped
# control does so explicitly (design 2.2, as isolation/sso/ci-token-basic/
# 5f2q-upload-scope/api-alias-repo-key do).
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
